{
 "id": "l24",
 "label": "24",
 "title": "Saying please",
 "summary": "Paki- and maki- request verbs, softening with ngâ and namán, and asking with maáarì bang",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Make a polite request with a paki- verb and a maki- verb",
  "Know which doer form each one needs (mo or ka)",
  "Recognise the tenses of paki- and maki- verbs",
  "Soften a request with ngâ or namán",
  "Ask politely with Maáarì bang…? and answer Opô, maáarì"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Two prefixes for please",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog has no single word for *please*. Instead, you add a prefix to an action root to make a special **request verb**. The two prefixes are **paki-** and **maki-**."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Paki- or maki-?",
     "items": [
      "A **paki-** verb is **passive**, like an **-in-** verb (Lesson 15). The doer is in the **possessive form**, such as [[niyá|poss]] and [[mo|poss]].",
      "A **maki-** verb is **active**, like an **-um-** verb (Lesson 14). The doer is in the **nominative form**, such as [[si|marker]] Nestor, [[ako|pron]], [[siyá|pron]] and [[ikáw|pron]].",
      "These verbs have tenses, but the **imperative** (command) form is the one mostly used.",
      "Tagalogs prefer to speak in the passive, so **paki-** verbs are used more widely for requests."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Paki- takes mo, maki- takes ka",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Paki-",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Pakikúha",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "mo",
         "r": "poss"
        },
        {
         "t": "ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "aking",
